{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "At fixed L, mu, n, and slip parameters, Delta_p depends on Q and R through a single smoothly saturating slip-reduction factor controlled only by the common shear scale (3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n, so the resistance reduction versus shear has one shared saturation shape across all admissible conditions.",
      "child_relation": "Retain the same shear-thinning/slip framework but relax the assumption that confinement alters slip saturation only through the saturation denominator. Instead, confinement also weakens the incremental slip compliance itself at high shear-confinement loading, producing an additional roll-off in the rate at which resistance keeps decreasing with Q at small R.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "Q",
        "R",
        "beta_slip",
        "kappa_confine_sat",
        "lambda_confine_sat",
        "n"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "When Q is increased at fixed L and mu, the pressure-drop reduction from slip grows more quickly at larger R but bends over earlier for smaller R; equivalently, comparing two radii at matched nominal shear scale, the smaller tube shows less incremental slip benefit than the parent predicts once confinement-loading is order one."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "shear_load",
        "expression": "(3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n",
        "role": "power-law wall-shear-scale proxy already driving the parent resistance law"
      },
      {
        "name": "confine_sat_load",
        "expression": "lambda_confine_sat*(3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n",
        "role": "dimensionless loading marking onset of confinement-modified slip-saturation effects"
      },
      {
        "name": "slip_strength_proxy",
        "expression": "beta_slip*mu*(3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n",
        "role": "dimensionless unsaturated wall-slip compliance scale in the parent mapping"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when slip is active and confinement loading is not negligible: beta_slip*mu*(3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n = O(0.1-10) and lambda_confine_sat*(3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n = O(0.1-10), especially for smaller R at moderate-to-high Q within laminar flow.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, moderate changes in Q and R reveal mild radius-dependent bending of Delta_p versus Q; in holdout, extending to smaller R or larger Q strengthens this extra curvature because confinement suppresses additional slip compliance itself, not just its saturation scale.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent when the added confinement weakening tends to zero, e.g. vanishing new modulation amplitude or in the low-loading limit lambda_confine_sat*(3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n << 1 where the extra compliance roll-off is inactive.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes confinement influences only where slip saturation occurs, while the magnitude of incremental slip compliance beta_slip remains unaffected by confinement once shear is specified.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Confinement-modified near-wall structure changes only the saturation denominator of slip, not the effective slip-compliance numerator.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "In strongly confined smooth-tube flow, near-wall structuring/depletion can both cap the saturation scale and reduce the marginal ability of increasing wall shear to generate further slip, so confinement acts on slip strength as well as on slip saturation onset.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "Q",
      "R",
      "beta_slip",
      "kappa_confine_sat",
      "lambda_confine_sat",
      "n",
      "mu"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: at fixed L and mu, increasing Q lowers the effective resistance through one slip-compliance branch whose numerator grows with shear and whose denominator saturates with a confinement-modified factor; all radius dependence enters through the same shear proxy (3*Q/(pi*R**3))**n, so one shared parameter set implies one common curvature pattern once plotted against that load. Proposed refinement: relax that idealization so confinement also attenuates the slip-compliance strength itself at high shear-confinement loading. Distinguishing condition: vary Q and compare two fixed radii while holding L, mu, n, beta_slip, gamma_slip_sat, kappa_confine_sat, and lambda_confine_sat common. In the child, the smaller-R case develops an earlier loss of incremental slip benefit, so Delta_p versus Q bends upward relative to the parent at high load; the larger-R case retains more of the parent-like slip reduction over the same Q range. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the predicted Delta_p(Q,R) surface changes directly. Admissible observability requires remaining laminar and using conditions where slip is already non-negligible and the confinement-loading factor reaches order unity; otherwise the child collapses back toward the parent and the contrast is weak."
  },
